WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:02.460
- He's the host of his own hit CNN show,

00:00:02.460 --> 00:00:04.030
United Shades of America.

00:00:04.030 --> 00:00:05.990
It's amazing, you should check it out.

00:00:05.990 --> 00:00:08.520
Let's welcome W. Kamau Bell.

00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:10.190
Hey, what's up Kamau, how you doing?

00:00:10.190 --> 00:00:11.080
- Hey, good.

00:00:11.080 --> 00:00:12.500
Thanks for having me.

00:00:12.500 --> 00:00:16.210
- Did you think, cause here
on the show we want this

00:00:16.210 --> 00:00:17.900
to be an active and open forum

00:00:17.900 --> 00:00:20.720
for everyone to share their
experiences and thoughts.

00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:25.040
But do you think that
what you've been doing is

00:00:25.040 --> 00:00:27.720
kind of what's happening right now?

00:00:27.720 --> 00:00:30.830
- I think the conversations
that I've been having

00:00:30.830 --> 00:00:32.150
on the United Shades,

00:00:32.150 --> 00:00:33.630
I've been hearing all my life

00:00:33.630 --> 00:00:35.803
from my mom, friends of my mom.

00:00:36.950 --> 00:00:39.750
My mom's a single mother,
which means as a kid,

00:00:39.750 --> 00:00:41.350
you go everywhere your single mom does

00:00:41.350 --> 00:00:42.810
cause she's not paying for babysitters.

00:00:42.810 --> 00:00:44.360
Doesn't have the money.

00:00:44.360 --> 00:00:47.830
So I was always around a
lot of adult conversations

00:00:47.830 --> 00:00:49.300
and I was born a few years

00:00:49.300 --> 00:00:52.310
after the civil rights
movement sort of peaked.

00:00:52.310 --> 00:00:54.650
So there's a lot of conversations
about what do we do next?

00:00:54.650 --> 00:00:56.773
So for me, this is just the conversation

00:00:56.773 --> 00:01:00.050
that I was always hearing,
and I feel really honored

00:01:00.050 --> 00:01:01.980
that CNN invites me to have them on TV

00:01:01.980 --> 00:01:04.260
because me and my friends
have these conversations

00:01:04.260 --> 00:01:06.709
all the time, but I know the CNN audience

00:01:06.709 --> 00:01:09.400
isn't always having those conversations.

00:01:09.400 --> 00:01:11.968
- We're starting the
conversation in our work family.

00:01:11.968 --> 00:01:15.040
How have you been starting the
conversation in your family

00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:16.960
about George Floyd and the protests?

00:01:16.960 --> 00:01:18.720
- Well, you know, because
I worked for the news,

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:20.080
we have the news on all the time.

00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:22.560
We just had it on before
I sat down with you,

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:23.500
and my kids ask questions.

00:01:23.500 --> 00:01:25.360
I got a nine year old and five-year-old

00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:27.190
and they ask questions
about what's going on.

00:01:27.190 --> 00:01:29.300
And the thing, I think a lot of people,

00:01:29.300 --> 00:01:30.960
especially white people are afraid of is,

00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:32.540
I don't want to show my kids the news

00:01:32.540 --> 00:01:34.880
cause I don't know how
they'll handle the information

00:01:34.880 --> 00:01:36.140
or I don't know what to tell them.

00:01:36.140 --> 00:01:38.300
But for me it's like, just
do the same thing you do

00:01:38.300 --> 00:01:39.133
with your kid any time.

00:01:39.133 --> 00:01:41.000
talk to them and answer the question,

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:42.490
and at some point your
kid will let you know

00:01:42.490 --> 00:01:44.237
when they've had enough,
cause they'll be like,

00:01:44.237 --> 00:01:45.210
"I don't understand."

00:01:45.210 --> 00:01:47.520
Or they'll just walk off
and go do something else.

00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:48.830
- Yeah, they just walk away.

00:01:48.830 --> 00:01:50.350
It's like an attention span of a gnat.

00:01:50.350 --> 00:01:52.980
Yeah, I agree.

00:01:52.980 --> 00:01:56.590
So many people that
I've talked to as well,

00:01:56.590 --> 00:01:59.200
in the limelight, not in
the limelight, you know,

00:01:59.200 --> 00:02:01.183
they're afraid to say the wrong thing.

00:02:03.130 --> 00:02:04.720
And sometimes I feel like
that's happened with me.

00:02:04.720 --> 00:02:05.627
Like I say something I'm like,

00:02:05.627 --> 00:02:09.600
"That is not what I meant
at all. Like, wait, what?"

00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:11.610
So what do you say to that?

00:02:11.610 --> 00:02:13.700
Cause I don't think people should be quiet

00:02:13.700 --> 00:02:15.250
cause that hasn't helped.

00:02:15.250 --> 00:02:18.260
But at the same time,
what if they're afraid

00:02:18.260 --> 00:02:21.800
that what they're saying
is going to be twisted.

00:02:21.800 --> 00:02:24.660
- I'd say don't be afraid
of saying the wrong thing.

00:02:24.660 --> 00:02:26.900
Be confident you're going
to say the wrong thing.

00:02:26.900 --> 00:02:28.853
You're going to say the wrong thing.

00:02:31.723 --> 00:02:33.940
It's the step into saying
the wrong thing boldly.

00:02:33.940 --> 00:02:34.773
And the key part is,

00:02:34.773 --> 00:02:36.567
when somebody tells you
said the wrong thing, go,

00:02:36.567 --> 00:02:38.530
"Oh, did I say the wrong thing?

00:02:38.530 --> 00:02:40.500
I'm sorry, what is the right thing?"

00:02:40.500 --> 00:02:41.780
Or, "Tell me where I can go find out

00:02:41.780 --> 00:02:43.300
what the right thing is and I will learn

00:02:43.300 --> 00:02:44.596
to say the right thing."

00:02:44.596 --> 00:02:47.230
I think people's fear of
saying the wrong thing again,

00:02:47.230 --> 00:02:49.060
white people, it then puts
them in a places where

00:02:49.060 --> 00:02:52.560
they never say anything, which
is why we have, you know,

00:02:52.560 --> 00:02:54.900
George Floyd obviously
is not the first person

00:02:54.900 --> 00:02:56.640
to be killed by a police.

00:02:56.640 --> 00:02:58.120
- Breonna Taylor. I mean, we can go on.

00:02:58.120 --> 00:02:59.580
- Yeah, there's Tony McDade.

00:02:59.580 --> 00:03:03.080
And there's been black
people since George Floyd

00:03:03.080 --> 00:03:05.360
who've been killed by
cops under circumstances

00:03:05.360 --> 00:03:07.100
that don't make any sense.

00:03:07.100 --> 00:03:09.880
But the fact is that I think
for the most part white people,

00:03:09.880 --> 00:03:11.950
because they're not
continuing the conversation,

00:03:11.950 --> 00:03:14.530
it's like every time
it happens, it's anew.

00:03:14.530 --> 00:03:16.890
It's like, "I can't believe
that happened," you know?

00:03:16.890 --> 00:03:17.723
- Yeah.

00:03:17.723 --> 00:03:20.190
- I think you have to
realize if you had invested

00:03:20.190 --> 00:03:22.960
in the conversation, let's
say if your great grandparents

00:03:22.960 --> 00:03:25.960
had invested in it during
when Emmett till was murdered,

00:03:25.960 --> 00:03:28.030
we wouldn't have to be
having the conversation now.

00:03:28.030 --> 00:03:30.990
- Honestly, this whole
conversation is why we've started

00:03:30.990 --> 00:03:33.470
the conversation specifically
in our own environment,

00:03:33.470 --> 00:03:36.260
because I think sometimes we
start thinking of a problem

00:03:36.260 --> 00:03:37.620
and it gets so big.

00:03:37.620 --> 00:03:39.090
Like when I think of world hunger,

00:03:39.090 --> 00:03:40.320
when I think of Black Lives Matter,

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:42.527
when I think of anything,
I just start going,

00:03:42.527 --> 00:03:44.810
"Oh, it's such a big problem,
how are we going to fix it?"

00:03:44.810 --> 00:03:47.220
But I think if you focus in
your own small community,

00:03:47.220 --> 00:03:48.700
and if everybody does that,

00:03:48.700 --> 00:03:50.210
then that's where change can happen.

00:03:50.210 --> 00:03:51.220
Because you start focusing,

00:03:51.220 --> 00:03:54.690
you start calling your friends
out, calling yourself out,

00:03:54.690 --> 00:03:56.760
allowing people to talk
and actually listening,

00:03:56.760 --> 00:03:57.593
like you're saying.

00:03:57.593 --> 00:04:00.140
And I think that's why we
wanted to start the conversation

00:04:00.140 --> 00:04:03.050
with our show and being the
change that we want to see.

00:04:03.050 --> 00:04:04.900
And I just want to
thank you because I know

00:04:04.900 --> 00:04:06.770
that you've been doing this for years

00:04:06.770 --> 00:04:09.203
because I've been one of your viewers.

00:04:10.340 --> 00:04:11.810
Well, y'all look for a season five

00:04:11.810 --> 00:04:14.040
of Kamau's Emmy winning
show, it's amazing.

00:04:14.040 --> 00:04:17.153
United Shades of America
this month on CNN.

